{0 S3 Guides}

Practical guides for using awskit-s3. See the {{!page-index}index page}
for the full library listing.

For adapter setup, request signing, and error handling, see
{{:../awskit/guides.html}[aws Guides]}. This page focuses on S3 operations.

{1 Buckets and Objects}

S3 organizes data into {b buckets} and {b objects}:

{v
  Bucket: my-bucket
  ├── photos/2024/vacation.jpg     ← object key
  ├── photos/2024/family.png
  ├── data/users.json
  └── config/settings.yaml
v}

A {b bucket} is a globally-named container. An {b object} is a file with a
key, a value (the bytes), and metadata. Keys are flat strings — the "/"
separators are conventional, not hierarchical. S3 has no true directories,
though prefix-based listing gives a similar effect.

{2 Bucket operations}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Bucket} provides:

{ul
  {li [create] — Create a new bucket}
  {li [delete] — Delete an empty bucket}
  {li [head] — Check if a bucket exists}
  {li [list] — List all buckets in your account}
  {li [get_location] — Get a bucket's region}
}

Each bucket can have sub-configurations accessed through submodules:
{ul
  {li [Bucket.Policy] — Access policy (JSON)}
  {li [Bucket.Versioning] — Object versioning (enable or suspend)}
  {li [Bucket.Encryption] — Default server-side encryption configuration}
  {li [Bucket.Tagging] — Key-value tags on the bucket itself}
}

{2 Object operations}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Object} provides:

{ul
  {li [put] — Upload an object}
  {li [get] — Download an object (optionally with byte range)}
  {li [delete] — Delete a single object}
  {li [delete_batch] — Delete up to 1000 objects in one request}
  {li [head] — Get object metadata without downloading}
  {li [copy] — Copy an object (server-side, no download needed)}
  {li [list] — List objects by prefix, with pagination}
  {li [Object.Tagging.get/put/delete] — Manage object tags}
}

{1 Result types}

Each operation returns a specific result type with the relevant data. All
operations return [(result, Error.t)] — no exceptions are thrown.

{2 Object.Put_result}

{[
type put_result = {
  etag : string;  (* ETag assigned by S3 *)
}
]}

{2 Object.Get_result}

{[
type get_result = {
  body : string;            (* the content *)
  etag : string;            (* e.g., "\"d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e\"" *)
  content_type : string option;
  content_length : int;
  last_modified : string;
  metadata : Metadata.t;     (* custom x-amz-meta-* headers *)
}
]}

{2 Object.Copy_result}

{[
type copy_result = {
  etag : string;         (* ETag of the new object *)
  last_modified : string; (* Last-modified timestamp of the copy *)
}
]}

{2 Object.List_result}

{[
type list_result = {
  keys : string list;
  is_truncated : bool;            (* more results available? *)
  next_continuation_token : string option;  (* pass to next call *)
}
]}

{2 Object.Delete_result}

{[
type delete_result = {
  deleted : Deleted.t list;  (* successfully deleted keys *)
  errors : Error.t list;     (* per-key failures *)
}
]}

Each [Deleted.t] has a [key] field. Each [Error.t] has [key], [code], and
[message] fields describing the failure.

{2 Object.Info}

From [head]:

{[
type info = {
  etag : string;
  size : int;
  last_modified : string;
  content_type : string option;
  storage_class : Storage_class.t option;  (* None = Standard *)
  metadata : Metadata.t;
}
]}

{1 Object operations in detail}

{2 Uploading (put)}

{[
let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.put conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"data.csv"
  ?content_type:(Some "text/csv")
  ?storage_class:(Some Standard_ia)
  ?metadata:[ ("author", "alice"); ("version", "2" ) ]
  ?cache_control:(Some "max-age=3600")
  ?content_encoding:(Some "gzip")
  ?content_disposition:(Some "attachment; filename=\"data.csv\"")
  file_content
]}

Optional parameters:
{ul
  {li [?if_none_match:true] — conditional write; returns
    [Error `Precondition_failed] if the key already exists}
  {li [?if_match:"\"etag...\""] — only write if the current ETag matches}
  {li [?content_type] — MIME type for the object}
  {li [?metadata] — custom key-value pairs stored as [x-amz-meta-*] headers}
  {li [?storage_class] — storage tier (Standard, Standard_ia, Glacier, etc.)}
  {li [?tags] — key-value tags attached to the object}
  {li [?cache_control] — Cache-Control header}
  {li [?content_encoding] — Content-Encoding header}
  {li [?content_disposition] — Content-Disposition header}
}

{2 Downloading (get)}

{[
let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.get conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"data.csv"
  ()
]}

Use [?range] for partial reads (see the {b Byte ranges} section below):
{[
(* Read the first 1024 bytes *)
let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.get conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large-file.bin"
  ~range:(Some (Awskit_s3.Range.Bytes (0, 1023)))
  ()
]}

Conditional reads with [?if_match], [?if_none_match],
[?if_modified_since], [?if_unmodified_since]:
{[
(* Only download if the ETag matches *)
let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.get conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"data.csv"
  ~if_match:(Some "\"d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e\"")
  ()
]}

{2 Checking existence (head)}

[head] returns [Ok (Some info)] if the object exists, [Ok None] if not —
no error for missing objects. This is the cheapest way to check whether an
object exists or to read its metadata without downloading the body:

{[
match Awskit_s3_eio.Object.head conn ~bucket:"b" ~key:"k" () with
| Ok (Some info) -> Fmt.pr "Size: %d" info.size
| Ok None -> Fmt.pr "Not found"
| Error e -> Fmt.epr "Error: %a" Awskit_s3.Error.pp e
]}

[head] also supports [?if_match], [?if_none_match], [?if_modified_since], and
[?if_unmodified_since] for conditional checks.

{2 Deleting objects}

Single delete:
{[
let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.delete conn ~bucket:"b" ~key:"k"
]}

S3 semantics: [delete] succeeds even if the key doesn't exist.

Batch delete (up to 1000 keys per request):
{[
let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.delete_batch conn
  ~bucket:"b"
  ~keys:["key1"; "key2"; "key3"]
in
match result with
| Ok { deleted; errors } ->
  (* deleted = list of successfully deleted keys *)
  (* errors = list of per-key failures with code and message *)
  ()
| Error e -> (* request-level failure *)
]}

{2 Listing objects}

{[
let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.list conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~prefix:"photos/2024/"
  ?delimiter:(Some "/")     (* group by "directory" *)
  ?max_keys:100
  ?start_after:(Some "marker-key")
  ()
]}

Paginate with [continuation_token]:
{[
let rec list_all conn ~bucket ~prefix acc continuation_token =
  let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.list conn
    ~bucket ~prefix ?continuation_token () in
  match result with
  | Error e -> Error e
  | Ok { keys; is_truncated; next_continuation_token } ->
    let acc = acc @ keys in
    if is_truncated then
      list_all conn ~bucket ~prefix acc next_continuation_token
    else Ok acc
]}

Optional parameters:
{ul
  {li [?delimiter] — group keys by prefix (e.g., ["/"] for directory-like
    listing)}
  {li [?max_keys] — page size (default 1000)}
  {li [?start_after] — start listing after this key}
  {li [?continuation_token] — continue from a previous truncated response}
}

{2 Copying objects}

Copy is server-side — S3 reads the source and writes the destination. Your
code never touches the data:

{[
let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.copy conn
  ~src_bucket:"source-bucket"
  ~src_key:"original.txt"
  ~dst_bucket:"dest-bucket"
  ~dst_key:"copy.txt"
  ?metadata_directive:(Some `Replace)
  ?metadata:[ ("copied-by", "backup-script" ) ]
  ()
]}

[?metadata_directive]:
{ul
  {li [None] — Copy metadata from source (default)}
  {li [Some `Copy] — Explicitly copy metadata}
  {li [Some `Replace] — Replace with new [?metadata]}
}

[copy] also supports [?if_match] and [?if_none_match] for conditional copies.

{1 Metadata}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Metadata} is a simple association list — custom key-value pairs
stored as [x-amz-meta-*] HTTP headers on S3 objects:

{[
let meta : Awskit_s3.Metadata.t = [ ("author", "alice"); ("version", "2") ]
]}

Set on upload, read from [get] and [head] results:

{[
(* Upload with metadata *)
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.put conn ~bucket ~key ?metadata:(Some meta) body

(* Read back *)
let { Awskit_s3.Object.Get_result.metadata } =
  Awskit_s3_eio.Object.get conn ~bucket ~key () |> Result.get_ok
in
(* metadata = [ ("author", "alice"); ("version", "2") ] *)
]}

{1 Tags}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Tag} represents a key-value pair attached to an object or
bucket:

{[
let tag : Awskit_s3.Tag.t = { key = "environment"; value = "production" }
]}

Tags are used for categorization, lifecycle rules, and access control.

{2 Object tags}

{[
(* Set tags on an object — replaces all existing tags *)
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Tagging.put conn ~bucket ~key
  [ { Tag.key = "env"; value = "prod" } ]

(* Read tags *)
let tags = Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Tagging.get conn ~bucket ~key

(* Remove all tags *)
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Tagging.delete conn ~bucket ~key
]}

{2 Bucket tags}

{[
(* Set tags on a bucket *)
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Tagging.put conn ~bucket
  [ { Tag.key = "team"; value = "platform" } ]

(* Read tags *)
let tags = Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Tagging.get conn ~bucket

(* Remove all tags *)
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Tagging.delete conn ~bucket
]}

{1 Byte ranges}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Range} represents a byte range for partial reads with
[Object.get]:

{[
(* Inclusive range from start to end *)
let range = Awskit_s3.Range.Bytes (0, 1023)

(* Everything from an offset onward *)
let range = Awskit_s3.Range.From 4096

(* The last N bytes *)
let range = Awskit_s3.Range.Suffix 1024
]}

{!val:Awskit_s3.Range.to_header} converts a range to an HTTP [Range] header value.
Returns [Error] if the range is invalid (e.g., negative offsets, start > end).

{1 Storage classes}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Storage_class} represents S3 storage tiers:
{ul
  {li [Standard] — General purpose, frequently accessed}
  {li [Standard_ia] — Infrequently accessed, lower cost}
  {li [Onezone_ia] — Infrequently accessed, single availability zone}
  {li [Intelligent_tiering] — Automatic optimization between frequent and
    infrequent tiers}
  {li [Glacier] — Archive, retrieval takes minutes to hours}
  {li [Glacier_ir] — Glacier with instant retrieval}
  {li [Deep_archive] — Long-term archive, retrieval takes hours}
  {li [Express_onezone] — High performance, single AZ}
}

Set on upload:
{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.put conn ~bucket ~key ?storage_class:(Some Glacier) body
]}

Read from object metadata:
{[
match info.storage_class with
| None -> (* Standard, the default *)
| Some Standard_ia -> (* infrequent access *)
| Some Glacier -> (* archived *)
| _ -> (* other tier *)
]}

{1 Multipart uploads}

For large objects (over 5 MB), use multipart uploads to upload in parts and
assemble them on S3. Each part must be at least 5 MB, except the last one.

{2 Three-step flow}

{[
(* 1. Initiate *)
let upload = Awskit_s3_eio.Multipart.create conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large-file.bin"
  ?content_type:(Some "application/octet-stream")
  ?metadata:[ ("source", "backup") ]
  ?storage_class:(Some Standard_ia)
  ()

(* 2. Upload parts *)
let part1 = Awskit_s3_eio.Multipart.upload_part conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large-file.bin"
  ~upload_id:upload.Awskit_s3.Multipart.Upload.upload_id
  ~part_number:1
  chunk1
in
let part2 = Awskit_s3_eio.Multipart.upload_part conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large-file.bin"
  ~upload_id:upload.Awskit_s3.Multipart.Upload.upload_id
  ~part_number:2
  chunk2
in

(* 3. Complete — S3 assembles the parts into the final object *)
let result = Awskit_s3_eio.Multipart.complete conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large-file.bin"
  ~upload_id:upload.Awskit_s3.Multipart.Upload.upload_id
  [ part1; part2 ]
in
]}

{2 Aborting}

If something goes wrong, abort the upload to clean up:
{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Multipart.abort conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large-file.bin"
  ~upload_id:upload.Awskit_s3.Multipart.Upload.upload_id
]}

Always abort uploads you don't complete — incomplete parts are still billed.

{2 Listing parts}

Check which parts have been uploaded so far:
{[
let parts = Awskit_s3_eio.Multipart.list_parts conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large-file.bin"
  ~upload_id:upload.Awskit_s3.Multipart.Upload.upload_id
in
(* parts : Part_info.t list — each has part_number, etag, size, last_modified *)
]}

{1 Presigned URLs}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Presigned} generates URLs that grant temporary access to S3
objects without requiring AWS credentials on the client side. The URL embeds
a SigV4 signature that expires after a configurable time.

Presigned URLs are generated {b purely} — no network call is made. The runtime
adapter versions ([Awskit_s3_eio.Presigned], [Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.Presigned]) pull the
region, credentials, clock, and host from the connection automatically.

{2 Download URL}

{[
let url = Awskit_s3_eio.Presigned.get_object conn
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"report.pdf"
  ?expires_in:3600   (* 1 hour, default *)
  ()
(* Anyone with [url] can download report.pdf for the next hour *)
]}

{2 Upload URL}

{[
let url = Awskit_s3_eio.Presigned.put_object conn
  ~bucket:"uploads"
  ~key:"user-file.csv"
  ?expires_in:300     (* 5 minutes *)
  ?content_type:(Some "text/csv")
  ()
(* Anyone with [url] can PUT to this URL for the next 5 minutes *)
]}

When [?content_type] is set on a PUT URL, the [Content-Type] is baked into the
signed headers — the uploader must send the same [Content-Type] or the
request will fail.

Expiry defaults to 3600 seconds (1 hour). Maximum is 604800 seconds (7 days).

{1 Bucket configuration}

{2 Bucket policies}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Policy} provides typed bucket policies with JSON
round-tripping and access evaluation. See the {b Policies in depth}
below.

{2 Versioning}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Bucket.Versioning} lets you enable or suspend object
versioning on a bucket. When versioning is enabled, S3 keeps multiple versions
of each object instead of overwriting.

{[
(* Enable versioning *)
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Versioning.put conn ~bucket:"my-bucket" Enabled

(* Check versioning status *)
let status = Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Versioning.get conn ~bucket:"my-bucket"
(* Returns: None (never enabled), Some Enabled, or Some Suspended *)

(* Suspend versioning — stops creating new versions but preserves existing ones *)
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Versioning.put conn ~bucket:"my-bucket" Suspended
]}

{2 Encryption}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Bucket.Encryption} manages default server-side encryption:

{[
(* Set AES-256 encryption *)
let config : Awskit_s3.Bucket.Encryption.Config.t = {
  rules = [ { sse_algorithm = Aes256; kms_master_key_id = None } ]
}
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Encryption.put conn ~bucket:"my-bucket" config

(* Set AWS KMS encryption *)
let kms_config : Awskit_s3.Bucket.Encryption.Config.t = {
  rules = [ {
    sse_algorithm = Aws_kms;
    kms_master_key_id = Some "arn:aws:kms:us-east-1:123456789:key/abc";
  } ]
}
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Encryption.put conn ~bucket:"my-bucket" kms_config

(* Read current encryption config *)
let config = Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Encryption.get conn ~bucket:"my-bucket"

(* Remove encryption config *)
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Encryption.delete conn ~bucket:"my-bucket"
]}

{1 Policies in depth}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Policy} provides typed bucket policies with JSON
round-tripping and access evaluation. A policy consists of {b statements},
each with an effect (Allow/Deny), a principal (who), actions (what), and
resources (where).

{2 Reading a policy}

{[
let policy = Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Policy.get conn ~bucket:"my-bucket"
]}

{2 Writing a policy}

Build a policy programmatically:
{[
let policy : Awskit_s3.Policy.t = {
  version = "2012-10-17";
  statements = [
    {
      effect_ = Allow;
      principal = Any;
      actions = [ "s3:GetObject" ];
      resources = [ Bucket_objects "my-bucket" ];
    }
  ]
}
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Policy.put conn ~bucket:"my-bucket" policy
]}

Or parse from JSON:
{[
let policy = Awskit_s3.Policy.of_json json_string
]}

And serialize back:
{[
let json = Awskit_s3.Policy.to_json policy
]}

{2 Principals}

{ul
  {li [Any] — matches all principals (the ["*"] wildcard)}
  {li [Aws arns] — matches specific AWS ARNs}
  {li [User ids] — matches specific user IDs}
}

{2 Resources}

{ul
  {li [Bucket "my-bucket"] — targets the bucket itself
    (ARN: [arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket])}
  {li [Bucket_objects "my-bucket"] — targets objects in the bucket
    (ARN: [arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*])}
}

Use {!val:Awskit_s3.Policy.Resource.to_arn} and {!val:Awskit_s3.Policy.Resource.of_arn}
to convert between the typed representation and ARN strings.

{2 Evaluating access}

{[
match Awskit_s3.Policy.evaluate policy ~access_key_id ~action:"s3:GetObject"
    ~target:(Object_target ("my-bucket", "secret.pdf"))
with
| `Allowed -> (* access granted *)
| `Denied  -> (* explicit deny in policy *)
| `No_match -> (* no matching statement, default deny *)
]}

Evaluation follows AWS semantics: explicit [Deny] always wins over [Allow],
and if no statement matches, the default is deny.

{2 Deleting a policy}

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Policy.delete conn ~bucket:"my-bucket"
]}

{1 Error handling}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Error} extends the base {!module:Awskit.Error} with S3-specific
variants:

{[
type t =
  [ Awskit.Error.base           (* all base errors *)
  | `Not_found               (* 404 - key doesn't exist *)
  | `Precondition_failed     (* 412 - if-match/if-none-match failed *)
  | `Bucket_already_exists   (* 409 - bucket name taken *)
  | `Bucket_not_empty        (* 409 - can't delete non-empty bucket *)
  | `No_such_bucket ]        (* 404 - bucket doesn't exist *)
]}

Pattern match on specific errors or handle them broadly:
{[
match result with
| Ok value -> (* success *)
| Error `Not_found -> (* key doesn't exist *)
| Error `Bucket_already_exists -> (* pick a different name *)
| Error (#Awskit.Error.base as e) -> (* any other AWS error *)
]}

Use {!val:Awskit_s3.Error.pp} to print errors for logging. Use
{!val:Awskit_s3.Error.of_aws_error} to widen a base AWS error to an S3 error.

{1 Simulation testing}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Sim} provides a deterministic in-memory S3 for testing. No
AWS account, no network, no flaky tests.

{2 Basic usage}

{[
let clock = Awskit_s3.Sim.Clock.create ()
let store = Awskit_s3.Sim.create_store ~clock ()
let credentials = Awskit_s3.Credentials.make
  ~access_key_id:"test" ~secret_access_key:"test" ()
let conn = Awskit_s3.Sim.connect store ~credentials

(* Use just like the real S3 client *)
Awskit_s3.Sim.Bucket.create conn ~bucket:"test" () |> ignore
Awskit_s3.Sim.Object.put conn ~bucket:"test" ~key:"hello" "world" |> ignore
]}

The simulated [conn] exposes the same operations as the real client —
[Object.put], [Object.get], [Object.list], [Bucket.create], [Policy], etc.
Write your code against the adapter interface and swap in [Sim] for tests.

{2 Deterministic clock}

The simulation clock doesn't advance on its own. You control time explicitly:
{[
let clock = Awskit_s3.Sim.Clock.create ~now:(Ptime.v (2024, 1, 1, ((0, 0, 0), 0))) ()
(* ... operations ... *)
Awskit_s3.Sim.Clock.advance clock (Ptime.Span.of_int_s 3600)
(* now it's 1 hour later *)
]}

Use {!val:Awskit_s3.Sim.Clock.advance_ms} for millisecond-grained control. Note
that it truncates to whole seconds with a minimum of 1 second.

This makes tests reproducible — the same operations at the same simulated
time always produce the same results. Deterministic time is especially
important for presigned URL expiry tests.

{2 Fault injection}

Inject specific faults to test error handling:
{[
Awskit_s3.Sim.inject_fault conn Slow_down
(* next operation returns `Service_unavailable *)

Awskit_s3.Sim.inject_fault conn Internal_error
(* next operation returns `Request_error (500, ...) *)

Awskit_s3.Sim.inject_faults conn [ Slow_down; Internal_error ]
(* next two operations will fault, in order *)

Awskit_s3.Sim.clear_faults conn
(* remove all injected faults *)
]}

Available faults:
{ul
  {li [Slow_down] — Returns [`Service_unavailable]}
  {li [Internal_error] — Returns [`Request_error (500, ...)]}
  {li [Connection_reset] — Returns [`Request_error (0, "connection reset")]}
  {li [Response_lost] — The operation happens but you get an error
    (simulates a network drop after S3 processed the request)}
}

[Response_lost] is particularly valuable — it tests the case where S3
successfully processes your request but you never see the response. This is
a real production failure mode that most SDKs don't make easy to test.

{2 Buggify mode}

Inspired by FoundationDB's testing methodology, buggify mode injects random
faults with a configurable probability:
{[
Awskit_s3.Sim.enable_buggify conn ~seed:42 ~prob:0.1
(* ~10% of operations will randomly fault, using seed 42 for reproducibility *)

Awskit_s3.Sim.disable_buggify conn
(* back to deterministic *)
]}

Use buggify in property tests: run the same operations many times with
different seeds and probabilities. The seed makes failures reproducible —
if a test fails with seed 42 at prob 0.1, it will fail the same way every
time.

{2 Operation history}

Audit all operations performed on the simulation:
{[
let history = Awskit_s3.Sim.history store
(* [ { op = `Put; bucket = "test"; key = "hello"; timestamp = ...; faulted = false } ] *)

Awskit_s3.Sim.clear_history store
(* reset the history *)
]}

Each record contains the operation type, bucket, key, timestamp, and whether
a fault was injected.

{2 Inspection helpers}

Peek into the simulation state directly (bypasses access checks):
{[
(* Check if an object exists and get its metadata *)
let meta = Awskit_s3.Sim.object_meta store ~bucket:"test" "hello"
(* Returns: Some { etag = "..."; size = 5; last_modified = ... } or None *)

(* List all keys in a bucket *)
let keys = Awskit_s3.Sim.keys store ~bucket:"test"

(* Dump all key-value pairs *)
let data = Awskit_s3.Sim.dump store ~bucket:"test"
]}

{2 Store configuration}

{!val:Awskit_s3.Sim.create_store} accepts an optional [?config] parameter:

{[
let store = Awskit_s3.Sim.create_store ~clock
  ~config:{ max_list_keys = 100 }
  ()
]}

[max_list_keys] controls the maximum number of keys returned by [Object.list],
enabling pagination testing.

{2 Multiple connections}

Multiple connections can share one store — each has its own credentials and
fault state, but they operate on the same data:

{[
let store = Awskit_s3.Sim.create_store ~clock ()
let conn1 = Awskit_s3.Sim.connect store ~credentials:admin_creds
let conn2 = Awskit_s3.Sim.connect store ~credentials:readonly_creds
(* conn1 and conn2 see the same buckets and objects *)
]}

This is useful for testing policy enforcement — different principals with
different credentials should get different access results.

{1 Connecting to LocalStack or MinIO}

Override the endpoint to point at a local S3-compatible service:
{[
(* Eio adapter with custom endpoint *)
let conn = Awskit_s3_eio.create
  ~env
  ~region:"us-east-1"
  ~credentials
  ~endpoint:(Awskit_s3.Endpoint.http ~host:"localhost" ~port:4566 ())
  ()
in

(* Now all S3 operations go to LocalStack *)
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.create conn ~bucket:"test" ()
]}

MinIO works the same way, typically on port 9000:
{[
let conn = Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.create
  ~region:"us-east-1"
  ~credentials
  ~endpoint:(Awskit_s3.Endpoint.http ~host:"localhost" ~port:9000 ())
  ()
|> Result.get_ok
]}

Pass an explicit {!module:Awskit_s3.Endpoint} when talking to LocalStack,
MinIO, or another S3-compatible service. Omit [?endpoint] for production AWS
HTTPS endpoints.

For the Lwt + Unix adapter, omitted arguments are read from standard AWS
environment variables by [awskit.unix]: [AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID],
[AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY], optional [AWS_SESSION_TOKEN], [AWS_REGION], and
[AWS_DEFAULT_REGION]. Endpoint overrides remain explicit.
